{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Counting copies in a construction is not a lower bound on every implementation. For a one-unit network, one copy per timestep gives an unrolling with T units. The TARGET says that no functionally equivalent feedforward system can use fewer, whether or not it is built by unrolling. Neither the copy count nor the qualified observation that feedforward networks generally need more neurons establishes that necessity."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The TARGET selects a particular function: a single initially false Boolean unit records whether any input so far has been true. Neither passage identifies this function or a one-unit example."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The TARGET specifies finite-horizon equivalence as exact output agreement on every Boolean list of length at most T, including the empty list, with no requirement on longer lists. The passages do not specify this test for functional equivalence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The TARGET counts state indices as neurons and defines feedforwardness by acyclicity of update dependencies tested on all state assignments, even unreachable ones. These are modeling choices, not criteria given in the passages."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The TARGET captures the author's proposed lower bound for the Boolean example, but that bound is an additional claim. The sources describe the size of the network produced by unrolling and a general tendency toward larger feedforward networks, not a minimum size for every equivalent network."
}
